	I installed nethack 3.1 on our network of Sun workstations
running SunOS 4.1.1, and with the network option enabled (or defined,
however you prefer to look at it).  Tonight I logged in and attempted
to play, and it said "Too many hacks running."  The MAXNRUSERS (or
whatever the macro is) in the nethack shell script is set to 4, and
when I see someone playing (with 'w') it indeed says "nethack 4."  I
did a 'w' at all three major machines, and found only one user running
nethack.  I "su"'d over to the nethack account (being game
administrator hath its privileges...) and did an "rsh ps" at every
single machine on the network that had a user on it.  Again, all I
found was that one user playing.
	My question is this:  If a user played, and then shelled out
(using !, for example) and started another process, would these games
be counted for the 4 allowed?  I just did an ls of the nethackdir and
there were indeed alocks, blocks, clocks and dlocks.  
	Also, how could I find these processes and kill them?  'ps
-aux' doesn't seem to show them, or I'd have sent them a "kill -9" a
while ago 8).
	I think I'll go modify the 'news' file to chastise people
about leaving games going -- I found someone with three shelled games
the other day.

	Argh.
